<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalisz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalisz is a city in Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on the Prosna river, often regarded as the oldest town in Poland and identified with the Calisia mentioned by Ptolemy. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of a Piast duchy, and there in 1264 Duke Bolesław the Pious issued the Statute of Kalisz granting privileges to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kalisz-1343">Treaty of Kalisz</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> was signed there in 1343.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalisz.jpg" alt="Kalisz" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sławomir Milejski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SM_Kalisz_S%C4%85d_2022(0).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kalisz lies on the Prosna river in the south-east of Greater Poland, the region regarded as the place where the Polish state began, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Poznań</a> is one of its two traditional capitals. Its name is thought to come from the archaic word kal, meaning swamp or marsh. Roman-era finds in the area suggest a stop on the Amber Trail to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations show an early medieval settlement of the Piast period, and the modern town was most likely founded in the 9th century as a castellany and minor fort. In 1106 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> captured it for his domain. Between 1253 and 1260 it was incorporated under the German town law of Środa Śląska, a variant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> law, and during the fragmentation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> it formed a separate duchy under a local branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>. </p><p>The Statute of Kalisz of 1264, issued by Bolesław the Pious, protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> at a time of persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and in later centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> became a destination for Jewish migration.  In 1314 Ladislaus the Short made it capital of the Kalisz Voivodeship. Casimir III the Great signed a peace treaty with the Order there in 1343.</p><p>Kalisz was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition</a> of 1793, regained by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> after the Greater Poland uprising of 1806 and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s fall, became a provincial capital of Congress Poland. In August 1914 German forces shelled the city and burned it to the ground, although the Russian troops had left without a fight; of about 68,000 inhabitants only 5,000 remained a year later, and much of the centre was rebuilt by the war's end.</p><p>After the German invasion of 1939 the city was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> executed Polish defenders in the city and at Winiary, and the Intelligenzaktion targeted the Polish intelligentsia. The Germans set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a> camp for Polish children taken from their parents. About 30,000 local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were murdered by the end of the war, and 20,000 local Catholics were murdered or expelled. The population in 1945 was 43,000, about half the pre-war figure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kalisz is the second-largest city of the Greater Poland Voivodeship, with 97,905 residents in December 2021, and is the seat of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Kalisz, created in 1992. It is a historical burial site of Piast dukes and medieval Polish monarchs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalisz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For much of the next century the duchy was contested between Mieszko's descendants and was at times divided between Poznan and <b>Kalisz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Duchy of Greater Poland</a></li></ul></section>
